# Xcode per-user state. Opening ios/TruePadApp in Xcode creates these; they are
# machine-specific (window layout, selected scheme, last device) and must never
# reach source control.
xcuserdata/
*.xcuserdatad
DerivedData/

# PHYSICAL-CEREMONY EVIDENCE. `scripts/cross-edition-physical.sh` collects a run
# into `artifacts/cross-edition/<timestamp>/` by default, and what it collects
# includes `02-transfer.tps2` — a SEALED PAD TRANSFER PACKAGE pulled off the
# iPhone — alongside both .xcresult bundles and the device logs. One ceremony run
# followed by a routine `git add -A` would put a sealed pad into history, where
# deleting it later does not remove it. PROVENANCE.md already says the evidence
# bundle "is not committed"; this is what makes that true rather than a habit.
artifacts/

# Xcode writes this into the VENDORED package when it resolves dependencies, and
# `ios/vendor/verify-vendor.sh` then fails: that script compares the vendored tree
# against pristine upstream plus the one reviewed patch, and an extra file is a
# difference. It is generated build debris, never tracked, and ignoring it keeps a
# routine archive from producing a supply-chain gate failure that looks alarming
# and means nothing. The app's OWN resolved pins are tracked separately, at
# ios/TruePadApp/TruePadApp.xcodeproj/project.xcworkspace/xcshareddata/swiftpm/Package.resolved.
ios/vendor/swift-crypto/Package.resolved
